"""
A pilot holding an attitude: pitch to a commanded angle, wings level.

# Why this is needed rather than a fixed stick

An aeroplane is not flown by holding the controls still. Three things in this
model make that concrete:

* The spiral mode is divergent --- mildly, doubling in about thirteen seconds,
  which is normal and which a pilot corrects without noticing.
* The propeller torque reaction rolls the airframe continuously, and nothing
  else opposes it, so a fixed stick guarantees a departure into a spiral.
* The phugoid is near neutrally damped, so a fixed elevator leaves the speed
  and the climb angle swinging for the whole flight.

Holding an attitude fixes all three at once, and it is what a pilot actually
does: a take-off is flown by raising the tail, rotating at a speed and then
holding a climb attitude, not by holding a stick position.

# The loops

Both are the same shape --- proportional on the attitude error, rate damping,
and an integral term:

    elevator = k_theta*((theta - theta_cmd) + xi_theta/T_i) + kd_theta*der(theta)
    aileron  = -k_phi*((phi - phi_cmd) + xi_phi/T_i)        - kd_phi*der(phi)

with the signs following the aircraft's own control conventions --- positive
elevator is trailing edge down and pitches the nose down, so the elevator gain
is positive on the error; positive aileron rolls right, so the aileron gain is
negative on it.

The proportional and rate gains are not fitted. With the pitch axis
approximated as `der(q) = M_q*q + M_delta*elevator`, the closed loop is a
second order system with `wn^2 = -M_delta*k_theta` and
`2*zeta*wn = -(M_q + M_delta*kd_theta)`, so measuring the two derivatives from
the model fixes the gains for a chosen bandwidth. The defaults here are about
1.3 rad/s and 0.9 critical on the motor glider. The roll axis is so heavily
damped by `L_p` that it is effectively first order, and `k_phi` sets a bank
correction time constant of roughly `-L_p/(L_delta*k_phi)`.

The integral terms are there because without them the commanded attitude is
not the attitude that results. Holding a climb needs a standing elevator
deflection, and a proportional loop can only produce it from a standing error:
on this aircraft that is three degrees of pitch droop, and on the roll axis
two thirds of a degree of bank --- which, being a bank, turns the aeroplane,
at `g*tan(phi)/V`. Both are real effects of a *fixed stick position*, but a
pilot is not a fixed stick: a pilot looks at the attitude and trims the error
away. `T_i` is the trimming time constant, and at four seconds it is far
slower than either loop, so it changes nothing about the response and only
removes the offset.

Both outputs are smoothly saturated at `delta_max`. The saturation is a
`tanh`, which costs a fraction of a percent of gain at small deflections and
cannot introduce a discontinuity for the solver to trip over. Each integrator
is wound at a rate scaled by `1 - (output/delta_max)^2`, which is exactly the
local gain of that `tanh`: the integrator slows as the control runs out of
authority and stops dead at full deflection. That is anti-windup with no extra
constant to choose, and it matters here --- the commanded ground attitude is
one the aircraft cannot reach until the elevator has some airspeed to work
with.

# Rotation is on speed, not on time

`theta_cmd` steps from `theta_ground` to `theta_climb` as the airspeed passes
`V_rotate`, blended over `V_blend`. Keying it to speed rather than to a clock
is what makes the take-off self-sequencing: change the mass, the thrust or the
rolling friction and the aircraft still rotates at the right speed instead of
at whatever point in the roll a fixed time happened to correspond to.

# What this is not

There is no speed hold and no throttle loop, so the aircraft climbs at
whatever speed the commanded attitude and the open throttle produce. There is
also no rudder, which is honest only because this airframe has no modelled
slipstream swirl and no P-factor --- the two things that make a real pilot
hold right rudder on take-off. Add either and this model needs a yaw loop.
"""
component Pilot
  "Mount on the aircraft datum; attitude and velocity are read from here"
  frame_a = Frame3D() {^frame_a}
  "Density and wind, so the loops work on airspeed rather than ground speed"
  air = AirState() {^air}
  "Elevator command, positive trailing edge down"
  elevator = RealOutput() {^elevator}
  "Aileron command, positive rolls right"
  aileron = RealOutput() {^aileron}

  "Pitch attitude held below the rotation speed; level is the tail-up attitude of a taildragger"
  parameter theta_ground::Angle = 0.0
  "Pitch attitude held above the rotation speed"
  parameter theta_climb::Angle = 0.12
  "Airspeed at which the aircraft is rotated"
  parameter V_rotate::Velocity = 22.0
  "Width of the blend between the two commanded attitudes"
  parameter V_blend::Velocity = 1.5
  "Bank angle held throughout, positive right wing down"
  parameter phi_cmd::Angle = 0.0
  "Elevator per radian of pitch error"
  parameter k_theta::Real = 0.25
  "Elevator per radian per second of pitch rate"
  parameter kd_theta::Real = 0.06
  "Aileron per radian of bank error"
  parameter k_phi::Real = 0.35
  "Aileron per radian per second of roll rate"
  parameter kd_phi::Real = 0.05
  "Time over which a standing attitude error is trimmed away, both axes"
  parameter T_i::Real = 4.0
  "Control deflection limit, both axes"
  parameter delta_max::Angle = 0.35
  "Airspeed regularisation, so the speed is differentiable at rest"
  parameter v_eps::Velocity = 0.5

  "Velocity of the datum relative to the air mass, world frame"
  variable v_rel_0::Velocity[3]
  "Airspeed"
  variable V::Velocity
  "Pitch attitude, positive nose up"
  variable theta::Angle
  "Bank angle, positive right wing down"
  variable phi::Angle
  "Commanded pitch attitude"
  variable theta_cmd::Angle
  "Pitch rate"
  variable theta_dot::Real
  "Roll rate"
  variable phi_dot::Real
  "Pitch attitude error"
  variable e_theta::Angle
  "Bank angle error"
  variable e_phi::Angle
  "Accumulated pitch error, trimmed out by the integral term"
  variable xi_theta::Real = initial 0.0
  "Accumulated bank error, trimmed out by the integral term"
  variable xi_phi::Real = initial 0.0
relations
  # A pilot is a sensor: it reads the frame and applies nothing to it
  frame_a.f = [0, 0, 0]
  frame_a.tau = [0, 0, 0]

  v_rel_0 = der(frame_a.r_0) - [air.v_wind_x, air.v_wind_y, air.v_wind_z]
  V = sqrt(v_rel_0[1]^2 + v_rel_0[2]^2 + v_rel_0[3]^2 + v_eps^2)

  # Column 2 of R is the world vertical resolved in body axes, which for the
  # yaw-pitch-roll sequence of this frame convention is
  # [sin(theta), cos(theta)*cos(phi), -cos(theta)*sin(phi)]. Reading the
  # attitude from it is exact for any heading and singular only with the nose
  # straight up, which no take-off reaches.
  theta = atan(frame_a.R[1, 2], sqrt(frame_a.R[2, 2]^2 + frame_a.R[3, 2]^2))
  phi = atan(-frame_a.R[3, 2], frame_a.R[2, 2])
  theta_dot = der(theta)
  phi_dot = der(phi)

  theta_cmd = theta_ground +
              (theta_climb - theta_ground) * 0.5 * (1 + tanh((V - V_rotate) / V_blend))

  e_theta = theta - theta_cmd
  e_phi = phi - phi_cmd
  elevator = delta_max * tanh((k_theta * (e_theta + xi_theta / T_i) + kd_theta * theta_dot) / delta_max)
  aileron = delta_max * tanh((-k_phi * (e_phi + xi_phi / T_i) - kd_phi * phi_dot) / delta_max)

  # Wound at the local gain of the saturation, so the integrator gives up
  # exactly as fast as the control runs out of authority
  der(xi_theta) = e_theta * (1 - (elevator / delta_max)^2)
  der(xi_phi) = e_phi * (1 - (aileron / delta_max)^2)
metadata {
  "Dyad": {
    "icons": {"default": "dyad://Multtest/Pilot.svg"},
    "labels": [
      {"label": "$(instance)", "x": 500, "y": 1100, "rot": 0},
      {
        "label": "theta = $(theta_climb)",
        "x": 500,
        "y": 1230,
        "rot": 0,
        "attrs": {"font-size": "90", "fill": "#6b7a88"}
      }
    ]
  },
  "_links": {
    "frame_a": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 300, "x2": 50, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "air": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": -50, "y1": 600, "x2": 50, "y2": 700}}}
    },
    "elevator": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 300, "x2": 1050, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "aileron": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"icon": {"x1": 950, "y1": 600, "x2": 1050, "y2": 700}}}
    }
  }
}
end
